# Spatial continuity

## Purpose

Keep the geography of a sequence coherent from panel to panel. Readers build
a mental map from where people stand and which way they move. A character who
fires from a platform they never climbed breaks that map, and a caption does
not mend it.

## Scope

Visual layer. Applies to scene blocking, page planning, the position fields
of the panel ledger, and every render that will be read next to another.

## Core Principles

- Every character in every panel has a stated position: level, side, and
  landmark.
- Directions are promises. Once a scene fixes which way travel and threat
  run, they keep running that way.
- Movement between levels is shown or declared. It never happens in silence
  between two panels.
- Geography is planned before generation. It is not patched afterwards.

## Positions

- Each cast row gives `position.level` (always), `position.side`, and
  `position.landmark` when the character is at one. Props that nobody holds
  get a position the same way.
- Levels, sides, and landmarks come from the location's `map.yaml`
  (`location-consistency.md`). "Near the door" is not a position unless the
  map has a door.
- `facing` records which way the character looks. Two characters in
  conversation face each other, and their sides agree with it.
- Positions distinguish foreground from background where it matters to the
  action. Say so in the panel's `framing.composition`.
- A character outside the frame keeps a position. Their row is marked
  `off_panel: true` and still says where they are.

## Travel and threat directions

- Each scene declares `geography.travel_direction` and
  `geography.threat_direction` when anything in it moves or approaches. On
  the ferry, the patrol launch always comes from the stern, screen right.
- Every panel of the scene respects both. A pursuer who came from the right
  does not arrive from the left on the next page.
- A reversal of travel reads as a return. Use it only when the story turns
  back.
- Eyelines follow the same geography: a character watching the threat looks
  toward the side it comes from, at a height that fits the levels involved.

## Changes of position

- When a character's level differs from their previous appearance in the
  scene, the change is shown in a panel, or the later row sets `moved` (how
  they got there), or the panel sets `ellipsis` (what the gutter skipped).
- The route exists on the map. Mara reaches the deck from the wheelhouse by
  the ladder, because `connections` says that is the way down.
- A change of side or landmark on the same level SHOULD be accounted for the
  same way when the reader would otherwise lose track of the character.
- Movement the story depends on gets a panel. If someone must be on the
  platform to fire from it, the reader sees them get there or sees them
  there before the shot.
- A character who was carried, bound, or badly hurt does not change position
  on their own without the state change being declared
  (`state-continuity.md`).

## Page and scene boundaries

- Pages chain through `continuity.enters_from` and `exits_to`. Positions in
  the first panel of a page follow from the last panel of the page before.
- Review pages together at boundaries. A page can be coherent alone and wrong
  against its neighbour.
- A new scene resets geography only by declaring a new location or a time
  jump. The first panel of a scene SHOULD show enough of the place to set
  the sides again.

## Validation

- `page.schema.json` requires `position` with a `level` on every cast row and
  on every placed prop.
- `scripts/check-continuity.js` reports a level change with no `moved` and no
  `ellipsis` (`POSITION_JUMP`, ledger rule 4), a broken page chain
  (`PAGE_CHAIN`, rule 10), and positions that name a level or landmark
  missing from the map (`UNKNOWN_REFERENCE`, rule 11).
- `hooks/detect-continuity-drift.js` is advisory and warns on write.
- The ledger stores where a character is. It does not store which way they
  are drawn moving, so no script checks travel and threat directions against
  the scene. Those are the `spatial_logic` check of the visual review.
- Whether a render honors the positions in the ledger is also visual review:
  the `position` and `movement` checks, filled in from the pixels, with the
  continuity-editor deciding doubtful cases.
